 "answer": "The `<#>` operator in pgvector returns the **negative inner product** of two vectors, as a `double precision` value.\n\nIt's negated so that `ORDER BY embedding <#> query LIMIT k` returns the most similar vectors first, since Postgres sorts ascending. To get the actual inner product, multiply the result by -1:\n\n```sql\nSELECT (embedding <#> '[3,1,2]') * -1 AS inner_product FROM items;\n```\n\nFor normalized vectors, the inner product equals cosine similarity. The related operators are `<=>` (cosine distance), `<->` (L2 distance), and `<+>` (L1 distance).",
